window.docContents[55].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Auth.Headline/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Auth___Headline":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth › Headline.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 561 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (50)notation \"PT\"def msgKeysTdef msgKeysVdef msgKeysOfVinductive FirstMsgPTagdef FirstMsgPTag.domdef FirstMsgPTag.coddef FirstMsgPTag.encdef FirstMsgPTag.decdef FirstMsgPTag.qCostabbrev AuthPTagdef AuthPTag.domdef AuthPTag.coddef AuthPTag.encdef AuthPTag.decdef AuthPTag.qCostdef pqAuthPrimIfacestructure ChallengerOracledef pqAuthChalIfacedef sigAndDdhOracledef saltKeyEncdef saltKeyEncCostdef msgKeyPrfOracledef hmacPrfOracleabbrev pqAuthSigAndDdhIfaceabbrev pqPrfMkIfaceabbrev pqPrfHmacIfacedef preKeyMsgTdef preKeyMsgVdef preKeyMsgOfVdef authQTdef authQencdef authRTdef authRdecdef authCodecdef pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδdef authPrimIfacePrimQueryWeightdef authChalIfacePrimQueryWeightdef authChalIfaceSigQueryWeightdef authChalIfaceDdhQueryWeightdef authGdhQueryBudgetstructure Auth1KeyReductionFamilystructure Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamilydef Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamily.evaldef authQueryKinddef authKindCountsstructure Auth1KeyCostedReductionBoundsstructure Auth1KeyReductionQueryBoundsdef PqxdhAuth1KeyReductionBoundδdef PqxdhAuth1KeySecureδ\n\n\n\nThe one-key first-message Auth statement (Lemma 1, second conjunct)Trusted: the statement. Every definition in this file is part of SignalPqxdh.Security.PqxdhAuth1KeySecureδ, the second conjunct of PqxdhSecureδ in Lemma 1 (SignalPqxdh.Security.PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ, Security/HeadlineDelta.lean). Read it alongside the game in Security/Auth/Game.lean, the shared ReductionDsl tables and budgets in Security/Headline.lean and CostedBudgets.lean, and KemCorrect.lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.This file extends the 11-tag handshake primitive interface with the 8 first-message ratcheting and AEAD operations (AuthPTag), defines the ReductionDsl challenger interfaces and authSpec S game codec over pqAtomicSig, and states that a costed seven-reduction family (sig, shcr, gdh, ck, mk, cpa, int) bounds Auth1KeyAdvantage by pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδ under both the uniform (stepCostBudget) and online/random-oracle split (stepCostBudgetSplit) step-cost budgets.\n\n\n\nChallenger interfaces and game codec\n\n\n\nnotation \"PT\"L50 ↗#\n\ndef msgKeysTL52–53 ↗#Representation of 80-byte MessageKeys (cipherKey, macKey, iv) in pqAtomicSig.\n\ndef msgKeysVL55 ↗#\n\ndef msgKeysOfVL56 ↗#\n\ninductive FirstMsgPTagL58–69 ↗#Tags for the 8 first-message primitive queries in SignalPqxdh.PrimQuery beyond the 11 handshake tags of PTag.\n\ndef FirstMsgPTag.domL71–78 ↗#\n\ndef FirstMsgPTag.codL80–87 ↗#\n\ndef FirstMsgPTag.encL89–97 ↗#\n\ndef FirstMsgPTag.decL99–103 ↗#\n\ndef FirstMsgPTag.qCostL105–113 ↗#Nominal byte-width query cost 1 + wd(dom) + wd(cod) of each first-message primitive tag.\n\nabbrev AuthPTagL115–116 ↗#Combined 19-tag primitive vocabulary (PTag ⊕ FirstMsgPTag) for Auth reductions.\n\ndef AuthPTag.domL118–120 ↗#\n\ndef AuthPTag.codL122–124 ↗#\n\ndef AuthPTag.encL126–128 ↗#\n\ndef AuthPTag.decL130–132 ↗#\n\ndef AuthPTag.qCostL134–136 ↗#\n\ndef pqAuthPrimIfaceL138–148 ↗#Primitive target interface on PrimBaseSpec for the shcr, ck, and cpa reductions.\n\nstructure ChallengerOracleL150–159 ↗#Challenger oracle specification over pqAtomicSig with nominal per-tag query costs.\n\ndef pqAuthChalIfaceL161–171 ↗#Target interface on PrimBaseSpec + C.spec over AuthPTag ⊕ C.Tag and pqAtomicSamplers.\n\ndef sigAndDdhOracleL173–183 ↗#Joint signing-and-DDH challenger oracle (sigAndDdhSpec): .inl m signs an m-byte message (qCost = 65 + m) and .inr () tests DDH on three 32-byte keys (qCost = 97).\n\ndef saltKeyEncL185–187 ↗#Maps an optional raw SPQR salt to the 32-byte PrfMsgKeys salt key via effectiveHkdfSaltBytes (DEV-LS25).\n\ndef saltKeyEncCostL189–190 ↗#Nominal 32-byte output cost of saltKeyEnc.\n\ndef msgKeyPrfOracleL192–202 ↗#Message-key PRF challenger oracle (prfMsgKeysSpec): encodes an optional s-byte salt via saltKeyEnc and returns 80-byte MessageKeys (qCost = 113 + s).\n\ndef hmacPrfOracleL204–214 ↗#HMAC PRF challenger oracle (prfHmacSpec): evaluates on an m-byte MAC input and returns a 32-byte tag (qCost = 33 + m).\n\nabbrev pqAuthSigAndDdhIfaceL216 ↗#\n\nabbrev pqPrfMkIfaceL217 ↗#\n\nabbrev pqPrfHmacIfaceL218 ↗#\n\ndef preKeyMsgTL220–222 ↗#Representation of PreKeyMsg (hdr, ephAPk, pqRatchetMsg, aeadCt) in pqAtomicSig.\n\ndef preKeyMsgVL224–225 ↗#\n\ndef preKeyMsgOfVL226–227 ↗#\n\ndef authQTL229–236 ↗#Sum-of-products representation of AuthQuery S (12 constructors) in pqAtomicSig.\n\ndef authQencL238–259 ↗#\n\ndef authRTL261–268 ↗#Sum-of-products representation of AuthResponse (12 constructors) in pqAtomicSig.\n\ndef authRdecL270–288 ↗#\n\ndef authCodecL290–297 ↗#Game codec for authSpec S over pqAtomicSig; codecCost = 32 bounds the constructor nesting depth and node count (≤ 21) of authQenc and authRdec.\n\n\n\nAdvantage bound under δ-correct KEM\n\ndef pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδL310–316 ↗#Advantage bound for Auth under a δ-correct KEM: pqxdhAuthBound plus a single kemDecapsFailureTerm S δ across all KEM decapsulations in the game.\n\nQuery weights and reduction families\n\ndef authPrimIfacePrimQueryWeightL320–321 ↗#Unit primitive-query weight on AuthPTag.\n\ndef authChalIfacePrimQueryWeightL323–326 ↗#Primitive-query weight on AuthPTag ⊕ ChalTag (1 on AuthPTag, 0 on ChalTag).\n\ndef authChalIfaceSigQueryWeightL328–331 ↗#Signing-query weight on pqAuthSigAndDdhIface (1 on .inr (.inl _), 0 otherwise).\n\ndef authChalIfaceDdhQueryWeightL333–336 ↗#DDH-query weight on pqAuthSigAndDdhIface (1 on .inr (.inr ()), 0 otherwise).\n\ndef authGdhQueryBudgetL338–349 ↗#Semantic DDH-query budget for the sig and gdh reductions in Auth: bilinear random-oracle-by-session consistency checks ((4 · M_ro + M_root) · M_sess) plus distinct session-pair checks (3 · M_sess · (M_sess - 1)), capped at 5 · q_total², together with q_total + N_dh + N_sess_tot final-extraction queries. Because only online queries create sessions, M_sess is clamped at min (min b.q_on b.q_total) S.N_sess_tot.\n\nstructure Auth1KeyReductionFamilyL351–362 ↗#Semantic reduction family for one-key first-message authentication and privacy: maps each b-bounded AuthAdversary S to adversaries against GapSigEufCma, KemShCr, GapDhWithSign, PrfChainKey, PrfMsgKeys, AeadIndCpa, and PrfHmac.\n\nstructure Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamilyL364–384 ↗#Syntactic ReductionDsl counterparts of Auth1KeyReductionFamily S over pqAtomicOps and authCodec S: sig and gdh target pqAuthSigAndDdhIface, mk and int target pqPrfMkIface and pqPrfHmacIface, and shcr, ck, and cpa target pqAuthPrimIface.\n\ndef Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamily.evalL386–430 ↗#Evaluates an Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamily S into a semantic Auth1KeyReductionFamily S with fuel b.q_total, default output false, signing budget 2 * min S.N_spk_tot S.N_recv, DDH budget authGdhQueryBudget S b, and PRF budgets 1 (ck) and S.N_recv_tot + 1 (mk, int).\n\ndef authQueryKindL432–437 ↗#Classifies AuthQuery into online queries (0, matching isAuthOnlineQuery) and offline random-oracle queries .roQuery and .roRootQuery (1) for per-kind step-cost accounting.\n\ndef authKindCountsL439–443 ↗#Per-kind query caps for a b-bounded AuthAdversary: min b.q_on b.q_total online queries (0) and min (b.q_ro + b.q_roRoot) b.q_total random-oracle queries (1).\n\nstructure Auth1KeyCostedReductionBoundsL445–520 ↗#Syntactic resource bounds for an Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamily S: operator and sampler size-honesty, uniform (stepCostBudget) and online/random-oracle split (stepCostBudgetSplit) step-cost bounds, worst-case query bounds over b.q_total handled queries, and run-local output block-size bounds via GRedBlockBounded / GRed2BlockBounded.\n\nstructure Auth1KeyReductionQueryBoundsL522–531 ↗#Semantic query-budget compliance for an Auth1KeyReductionFamily S: every component reduction adversary satisfies its .Bounded predicate for all b and A.\n\nOne-key Auth security statement\n\ndef PqxdhAuth1KeyReductionBoundδL535–550 ↗#Advantage-reduction bound for R : Auth1KeyReductionFamily S: for every δ-correct well-formed primitive suite P and b-bounded adversary A, Auth1KeyAdvantage S P A.1 is bounded by pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδ evaluated at the seven component advantages of R.\n\ndef PqxdhAuth1KeySecureδL552–562 ↗#One-key first-message authentication and privacy at setup S under a δ-correct KEM: there exists a costed reduction family R : Auth1KeyCostedReductionFamily S that satisfies syntactic resource bounds, semantic query bounds, and PqxdhAuth1KeyReductionBoundδ S R.eval.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth/Headline.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Auth.Headline/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Auth___Headline"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.CKA.LibsignalCKA/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___CKA___LibsignalCKA":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/CKA › LibsignalCKA.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 75 of 140 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDiffie–Hellman CKA over an abstract DH function and X25519 hook modelTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Generalizes ACD19 §4.1.2's Diffie–Hellman CKA from an F-module (CKA/DDHDefs.lean) to an abstract Diffie–Hellman function DHFun (dhScheme), and specifies the relation X25519Model connecting D to the binary's X25519 agreement and public-key hooks (clamp256, x25519Base).\n\n\n\nstructure DHFunL33–42 ↗#Abstract Diffie–Hellman function: base point base and scalar action dh : Sc → Pt → Pt.DEV-CKA10 (libsignal): X25519 is not a prime-order group. ACD19 §4.1.2 builds the CKA from a cyclic group. X25519 clamps its 32-byte scalars (exponents are not uniform modulo the group order) and uses x-only points. So the scheme is defined over an abstract DH function, and Thm 3's assumption becomes DDH for D (clamped exponents and x-only points), a non-standard assumption. Effect: none on libsignal_binary_e2e, which does not use Thm 3.\n\n\n\ndef CommutesL48–50 ↗#Commutativity on generated public keys: dh a (dh b base) = dh b (dh a base).\n\n\n\ndef dhSchemeL64–86 ↗#Diffie–Hellman ratchet CKA (ACD19 §4.1.2) over D : DHFun Sc Pt: CKA-S(h; x) = (x, dh x base, dh x h) and CKA-R(x, T) = (T, dh x T), returning 0 on out-of-order states (DEV-CKA9).DEV-CKA11 (libsignal): key generation when receiving. ACD19 Fig. 8 draws a party's next CKA coins when it next sends. libsignal draws the next sending key pair, and runs both root-KDF steps, when it receives a new ratchet key (dh_ratchet_step, double_ratchet.rs:289–291). The CKA syntax here stays ACD19's; the session model handles the difference (DEV-LS1). Effect: none here (see DEV-LS1 for the effect on the session game).DEV-CKA17 (libsignal): the initial CKA key is Bob's signed pre-key. ACD19 Fig. 8 starts from a fresh secret CKA key k_CKA. In libsignal, x₀ is the secret of Bob's signed pre-key (SPK): medium-term, shared by Bob's sessions until rotation, and also used by the handshake PQXDH. The model samples a fresh x₀ per session. Effect: out of scope (PQXDH; see DEV-LS22).\n\nnotation \"GameM\"L92 ↗#\n\nThe X25519 model\n\ndef x25519BaseL98–99 ↗#X25519 base point u = 9 as 32 little-endian bytes (RFC 7748 §4.1).\n\ndef clamp256L101–141 ↗#RFC 7748 §5 X25519 scalar clamping on a 256-bit word: clear bits 0–2 and 255, set bit 254.\n\nstructure X25519ModelL105–138 ↗#Hypothesis relating an abstract DHFun D (via an injective 32-byte point encoding enc) to the binary's X25519 agreement hook x25519 and public-key derivation hook pubKey.DEV-CKA12 (libsignal): only prime-order points. ACD19's group has no invalid elements. libsignal accepts any 32 bytes as a public key (twist, low-order and non-canonical u). Pt holds only prime-order-subgroup points, so the session game refuses injected ratchet keys outside Pt. Effect: restricts the adversary (DEV-LS5).DEV-CKA13 (libsignal): corruption leaks the raw coins. The CKA state holds the raw 256-bit coins x; libsignal stores, and a compromise reveals, the clamped bytes clamp256 x (curve25519.rs:34, private_key_bytes). The raw coins determine the clamped ones, so the model's adversary learns at least as much. Effect: none (conservative).DEV-CKA16 (machine model): ideal randomness. The coins x are uniform 256-bit strings: the caller's random number generator (libsignal's CryptoRng) is assumed perfect. ACD19 also assumes uniform coins. Effect: none; the generator's machine code is hooked, not verified (DEV-E8).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/CKA/LibsignalCKA.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.CKA.LibsignalCKA/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___CKA___LibsignalCKA"}});